  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1252 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nThe large swirling upper low over the central Plains is hard to\nmiss when you take a look at our weather picture this morning.\nAhead of that low is rich east Pacific and Gulf moisture through\na deep column with PWAT values over 2 inches producing areas of\nrain across eastern Kansas and Oklahoma. A combination of jet\nlevel and frontal ascent should keep a fairly broad area of rain\nand embedded showers and a storm or two going. These will move\nover the area tonight and through the day Friday. Peak rain\nchances look to be Friday morning, but expect at least some\nlingering rain to trail behind the cold front that should get\nthrough the area by midday tomorrow. Instability continues too\nlook too meager for severe weather threats, and rainfall\ncoverage and intensity look too limited for much flood risk\ngiven the antecedent conditions over the area.\n\nHighs behind the front fall into the mid 70s. A reinforcing\nfront moves through Monday bringing drier and slightly cooler\nair into the area which should help morning minimum temperatures\nfall into the 40s. A little bit of a trend to warmer\ntemperatures looks plausible towards the end of next week but\nnothing to the degree we are seeing today.",
